{"name":"Venetian Glass","hex":"#9cb08a","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"venetian","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Ellipsis of Venetian blind or alternative letter-case form of Venetian."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/venetian"},{"term":"glass","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An amorphous solid, often transparent substance, usually made by melting silica sand with various additives (for most purposes, a mixture of soda, potash and lime is added).","Any amorphous solid (one without a regular crystal lattice)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To fit with glass; to glaze.","To enclose in glass."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/glass"}],"entries":[{"title":"Venetian glass","blocks":[{"text":"Venetian glass is glassware made in Venice, typically on the island of Murano near the city. Traditionally it is made with a soda–lime \"metal\" and is typically elaborately decorated, with various \"hot\" glass-forming techniques, as well as gilding, enamel, or engraving. Production has been concentrated on the Venetian island of Murano since the 13th century. Today Murano is known for its art glass, but it has a long history of innovations in glassmaking in addition to its artistic fame—and was Europe's major center for luxury glass from the High Middle Ages to the Italian Renaissance. During the 15th century, Murano glassmakers created cristallo—which was almost transparent and considered the finest glass in the world. Murano glassmakers also developed a white-colored glass that looked like porcelain. They later became Europe's finest makers of mirrors."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Bowl_MET_DP100236.jpg/330px-Bowl_MET_DP100236.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venetian_glass","wikidata":"Q2116707"},{"title":"Glass","blocks":[{"text":"Glass is an amorphous (non-crystalline) solid. Because it is often transparent and chemically inert, glass has found widespread practical, technological, and decorative use in window panes, tableware, and optics. Some common objects made of glass are named after the material, e.g., a \"glass\" for drinking, \"glasses\" for vision correction, and a \"magnifying glass\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Fassade_Wilhelmstrasse_65%2C_Berlin-Mitte%2C_160417%2C_ako.jpg/330px-Fassade_Wilhelmstrasse_65%2C_Berlin-Mitte%2C_160417%2C_ako.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glass","wikidata":"Q11469"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Vetro_di_murano%2C_vaso_biansato%2C_vetro_trasparente_blu%2C_fine_xvi-inizio_xvii_sec.jpg/500px-Vetro_di_murano%2C_vaso_biansato%2C_vetro_trasparente_blu%2C_fine_xvi-inizio_xvii_sec.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Vetro_di_murano%2C_vaso_biansato%2C_vetro_trasparente_blu%2C_fine_xvi-inizio_xvii_sec.jpg/500px-Vetro_di_murano%2C_vaso_biansato%2C_vetro_trasparente_blu%2C_fine_xvi-inizio_xvii_sec.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Murano vase, around 1600, Hermitage Museum","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVetro_di_murano%2C_vaso_biansato%2C_vetro_trasparente_blu%2C_fine_xvi-inizio_xvii_sec.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Leguna_Veneta.png/500px-Leguna_Veneta.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Leguna_Veneta.png/500px-Leguna_Veneta.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Venice and Murano","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALeguna_Veneta.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.